AIM

To implement basic operations on dictionaries in Python such as accessing values, displaying keys and values, checking length, modifying entries, copying dictionaries and iterating through dictionary elements.

Algorithm

Begin the program.
Create a dictionary called student_info containing student details.
Create another dictionary called subject_marks containing marks of different subjects.
Display a value from the dictionary using its key.
Use the get() method to retrieve another value.
Print all keys present in the dictionary.
Print all values stored in the dictionary.
Display all key–value pairs using items().
Update one of the values in the dictionary.
Create a duplicate copy of the dictionary.
Print the length of the dictionary.
Traverse the dictionary using a loop and display its elements.
Terminate the program.

SOURCE CODE
student_info = {"name":"Shivani","age":20,"course":"Data Science","city":"Delhi"}

subject_marks = {"maths":82,"science":91,"english":75,"history":88}

print("Name:", student_info["name"])

print("Age:", student_info.get("age"))

print("Keys in dictionary:", student_info.keys())

print("Values in dictionary:", student_info.values())

print("Key value pairs:", student_info.items())

print("Dictionary length:", len(student_info))

student_info.update({"age":21})
print("After update:", student_info)

print("Copied dictionary:", student_info.copy())

print("Subjects:", subject_marks.keys())

print("Marks:", subject_marks.values())

print("Displaying dictionary content:")
for k in student_info:
    print(k, student_info[k])
